.TH CEVENTLOOP_REMOVE 3 "2026-09-21" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ceventloop_remove \- deregister a selectable from an event loop
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval ceventloop_remove(ceventloop loop, ceventloop_reg reg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Unregisters reg. You can call it from a callback of the registration
being removed (removing itself on an error is a common pattern), from
any other thread, and while a dispatch of the same registration is in
flight.

Once this call returns, no further callback of the registration runs.
That includes a callback the loop has collected but not started yet,
such as one waiting in a dispatch worker's queue when
num_reactor_threads > 1. The call does not wait, though, for a dispatch
that is already in flight when it is made.

A dispatch in flight can use whatever arg points to, so a caller that
has no other way to know when that memory is safe to free needs
handlers.on_removed (see ceventloop_add(3)). It fires exactly
once, asynchronously, and only when no dispatch of this registration
and no other ceventloop call that resolves it can be in progress.
A caller that is sure no dispatch can be in flight at the moment of the
removal may free arg at once, without on_removed. Removing a
registration from its own callback is one such case, and a registration
that the loop never dispatched is another.

The call doesn't close an fd and doesn't affect the lifetime of a
queue; it only releases the ceventloop's own bookkeeping for the
registration, in line with the "the caller owns the fd" contract of
cselectable_fd.

Remove every registration of an fd before you close it, as libevent and
libuv require too. When the last one is removed, the loop has stopped
watching the fd by the time this call returns, and the fd number is
free. A new registration of the same fd, or of a new descriptor that
reuses the number, is independent of the removed one: Nothing the loop
does later for the removed registration (a dispatch in flight, or the
later release of its bookkeeping) changes how the new one is watched.
A registration whose fd is closed first holds on to the number until it
is removed. Until then, a ceventloop_add(3) of a descriptor that
reuses the number fails with ccol_not_permitted for the direction the
stale registration holds, and can fail with ccol_unexpected_failure for
the other direction.

For a queue or a cchan selectable, the loop finishes its own
bookkeeping for the queue before this call returns: It unlinks the
registration's waiter from the queue and releases the registration.
A dispatch already in flight is a different matter, because its
callback holds the selectable the loop gave it and reaches the queue
through it. Unlike arg above, the queue needs no on_removed handshake
for that, because destroying the queue waits until every such callback
is done with it (see ccircq_destroy(3)). The usual order
of drain, remove and destroy is therefore safe from any thread, as long
as that thread is not itself running one of this registration's
callbacks.

Calling this function several times on the same reg is safe, whether
the calls come one after another or really run at the same time on
different threads. After a removal, reg is also still safe to pass to
ceventloop_modify(), ceventloop_pause()/_resume(),
ceventloop_reg_generation() or ceventloop_remove(). reg is
resolved against its loop's registration table before anything is
dereferenced, so a stale or removed handle is always caught and there
is no risk of a use-after-free.

The two kinds of repeated call return different values. A call made
strictly after an earlier ceventloop_remove() on the same reg has
returned finds that reg_h doesn't resolve at all, because the earlier
call released reg's slot before returning, so it gets
ccol_invalid_args, the same result that ceventloop_modify(),
_pause(), _resume() and ceventloop_reg_generation() give for a
removed reg. A call that really races another ceventloop_remove()
on the same reg can resolve it, in the narrow window before the other
call has released reg's slot; it then sees that reg is already marked
as removed, returns ccol_success and does nothing.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop  ceventloop the registration belongs to.
reg   Registration to remove.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success        The registration is removed, by this call or by
                    another call on the same reg running at the same
                    time (see DESCRIPTION).
ccol_invalid_args   loop is CEVENTLOOP_NULL, a stale handle,
                    or a handle that has already been destroyed; or
                    reg is CEVENTLOOP_REG_NULL or fails to resolve
                    for another reason, such as an earlier
                    ceventloop_remove() call that has already
                    returned.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call from any thread, including while a dispatch of the same
registration runs and from that registration's own callback.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void on_readable(ceventloop loop, ceventloop_reg reg,
                 cselectable *sel, void *arg) {
    conn_ctx_t *ctx = (conn_ctx_t *)arg;
    char buf[512];
    ssize_t n = read(sel->fd, buf, sizeof(buf));
    if (n <= 0) {
        ceventloop_remove(loop, reg);  /* self-removal, safe */
        close(sel->fd);
        free(ctx);
        return;
    }
    /* ... */
}
.fi
